\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} implemented. Revised on 2026-11-24 after the last change of that day: the default set is the set of the wave-6 integration plus \code{eager}, \code{skipargs} and \code{congruence}, and the machine with this set is the default engine. \code{convexpect} was added on 2026-11-24 and is off. Revised on 2026-11-25: the default budget of a probe of \code{eager} is 2^17 ticks, where it was 2^22. The list in full, with every gate and table, is [the switch reference](design-switches-document), which is kept as it was written and still names 2^22.}

\p{\strong{The rule.} Every optimisation of the machine that changes \em{what is computed} sits behind a staging switch: one bit of a per-engine set, tested where the optimisation would act. With no switch the machine is \strong{v0}: the generic tier, the complete procedures, a session of one declaration. A strict engine, which the driver uses for the cold re-check of a rejection, runs with no switch whatever is configured. So there is always a way back, and the differential gates can compare any set with v0 and with the reference engine. One document, the switch reference, is the single list: name, bit, owner, state, and the gate and the measurement that justify the state.}

\p{\strong{Where the stages come from.} The instruction set orders the work as a correctness gate followed by steps, each merged only when its gate is recorded: v0; (1) sessions, gated tabling, shared check facts, expected-type checking; (2) static facts; (3) code blocks for application inference and matching of domains; (4) code blocks for bodies and rules, and case trees; (5) speculative probes; (6) parallel workers. The switches are these steps cut finer. What the measurements did to the plan: the inference blocks of step 3 are built and off, and what is official-exact in the fact records of step 2 and the probes of step 5 costs more than it saves; those two are on only because the two proof-comparison shortcuts, which are not official-exact, need them.}

\p{\strong{How a set is given.} On the launcher \code{\verb~|-X machine.switches=LIST~}. The list is read from left to right starting from the empty set: a name or \code{+name} adds a switch, \code{-name} removes it, \code{none} empties the set, \code{default} adds the default set, \code{all} adds the staging switches of the design except \code{k2}. Without the setting an engine runs the default set. A switch that needs another one brings it, whatever the order of the list: \code{skipargs} brings \code{facts}, \code{congruence} brings \code{probes}. Examples: \code{none} is v0; \code{\verb~|default,-same~}; \code{\verb~|default,-eager,-skipargs,-congruence,-facts,-probes~} is the set of wave 6. The module's own tests run with \code{none}, so they do not move when a default changes.}

\p{\strong{The default set}: \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,bodies,pchk,match,joint,code,casetrees,lazycases,directrules,lazyeval,same~}, the set of the wave-6 integration (see [that decision](dec-LZFX) for its gates and numbers), plus \code{\verb~|eager,skipargs,congruence~} with \code{facts} and \code{probes}, since 2026-11-25 02:55 UTC, which is the evening of 2026-11-24 local time. See [the decision](dec-YP0N) for the rule and the table that chose it and [the changelog entry](log-HS1E) for its gates.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Switch}\th{What it turns on}\th{State}}
\tr{\td{\code{warm}}\td{a session survives an accepted declaration, see [warm sessions](design-warm-sessions)}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{gated}}\td{evaluation tables a node only if it is shared, a binder body or invariant, or lies deep in an inert nest, see [evaluation and tabling](design-evaluation-and-tabling)}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{bodies}}\td{the body of a beta step and a telescope's domains and results are evaluated without a tabling record}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{expect}, \code{pchk}, \code{match}, \code{joint}}\td{the four [inference shortcuts](design-inference-shortcuts)}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{code}, \code{casetrees}}\td{code blocks for bodies and recursor rules; case trees for the casesOn and matcher shapes, see [code objects and tiers](design-code-objects-and-tiers)}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{lazycases}}\td{the casesOn tree as the unfolding step of lazy delta}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{directrules}}\td{a recursor rule of the plain form is one application of the minor premise}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{lazyeval}, \code{same}}\td{two additions to [conversion](design-conversion)}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{facts}}\td{fact records; without \code{skipargs} the one consumer is the static answer to "is this a proof"}\td{on, brought by \code{skipargs}; alone it is official-exact and costs 1.0 to 2.1 \% of CPU time}}
\tr{\td{\code{probes}}\td{reordered argument comparison under budgets}\td{on, brought by \code{congruence}; alone it is official-exact and costs 1.4 to 3.2 \%}}
\tr{\td{\code{skipargs}}\td{an argument comparison skips proof positions and positions the definition does not read, see [the proof-comparison shortcuts](design-proof-comparison-shortcuts)}\td{on since the evening of 2026-11-24; not the official algorithm, its accepts are of the tolerated kind; never in \code{all}}}
\tr{\td{\code{congruence}}\td{the same-head probe, closure congruence and proofs by type, as speculative segments; a cap of 2^18 ticks per declaration, see [the changelog entry](log-9636)}\td{on since the evening of 2026-11-24; tolerated kind; never in \code{all}}}
\tr{\td{\code{tele}}\td{code blocks for application inference}\td{off: no corpus gains, the Cedar slice loses 2 to 6 \%}}
\tr{\td{\code{k2}}\td{derived rule trees for hot definitions}\td{off, see [the decision](dec-MOCU)}}
\tr{\td{\code{t2}}\td{hot code blocks are offered to the Truffle tier}\td{off, see [the decision](dec-ADIK)}}
\tr{\td{\code{eager}}\td{a declaration that enters [eager reduction](design-eager-reduction) stays on the machine; the probes of its plain phase have a budget of 2^17 ticks (2^22 until 2026-11-25), see the paragraph on the probe budget below}\td{on since the evening of 2026-11-24; without it the decline rule of [the decision](dec-X2UF) applies}}
\tr{\td{\code{convexpect}}\td{expected-type checking of a lambda also when its domain is only convertible with the expected one, as before the typed-binding fix}\td{off: no gain in warm wall clock, see [the decision](dec-0DSI)}}
\tr{\td{\code{levels-complete}}\td{universe levels compared by the complete relation instead of the official algorithm}\td{off by the maintainer's decision, never a default, see [the decision](dec-P8XP)}}
\tr{\td{\code{m2b}}\td{a direct-mapped memo for applications; one of the experiment bits each package has}\td{off, undecided}}
}

\p{\strong{The probe budget of \code{eager}.} A probe of the plain phase runs as a speculative segment under a tick budget, see [the changelog entry that introduced it](log-P6FV). Since 2026-11-25 the default budget is 2^17 ticks; it was 2^22, a value chosen so that the machine declined nothing it could decide. The sizes of the probes of the eight corpora fall into three groups, the same with one worker, with 8 workers and with sessions of one declaration: below 1,600 ticks; 34,096 to 63,654 ticks; 458,881 ticks and more. The budget lies in the upper gap, 2.06 times above the largest probe that completes and 3.5 times below the smallest it cuts. Exactly five units change. Four proofs of \code{Batteries.Data.Char.Basic}, on cslib and on Mathlib, whose probes completed after 0.98 to 3.2 M ticks each, are now cut: they are declines of the machine, and the reference engine decides the four in 15,760 steps. The one probe of con-leche that never completes (finding INT7-F1) is cut earlier. With one worker the machine's declines go from 14 to 22 attempts on cslib and from 120 to 128 on Mathlib, and the units the reference engine decides from 7 to 11 and from 60 to 64; no other unit of the eight corpora differs in ticks or outcome. See [the report](rep-OPT-I), sections 2.2 and 2.3, [the three sizes](ins-N4ES), [the changelog entry](log-I3WM) and [the decision of the second wave](dec-RHRE).}

\p{\strong{Not in the main tree.} A switch \code{closedpairs}, a table of comparison results for closed values that survives the machine's sessions, was built as a prototype on 2026-11-25 and was not adopted: it removes 4.5 to 15.1 \% of the ticks with 8 workers and gives no gain in time that the guard resolves on navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib, and the adversarial pass that [the rule for a default](dec-QN1O) requires was not made. The table above therefore has no row for it. Its 78 hostile exports are rows of the hostile suite, see [the changelog entry](log-T6DI). See [the report](rep-OPT-H) and [the decision of the second wave](dec-RHRE).}

\p{\strong{What is not a switch.} Two things that look like switches are deliberately something else. The rule that declines a declaration entering eager reduction holds in every configuration without \code{eager}, v0 included: it is a correctness rule. (Until the evening of 2026-11-24 the two proof-comparison shortcuts were a third: they sat behind a system property, so that no switch set could turn them on by accident. They are named switches now; \code{all} still does not contain them, and the property is still read for old command lines.) And the session cap, the tick bound per unit (\code{\verb~|-X machine.unit-ticks~}, default \code{\verb~|2^27~}; it makes the two arena tests on which the official checker does not terminate decline within seconds) and the host depth bound (\code{\verb~|-X machine.hostDepth~}, default 128) are settings with values, each with its own measurement; so are the probe budget of \code{eager} (\code{\verb~|-X machine.eager-probe~}, default \code{\verb~|2^17~}) and the cap on congruence segments (\code{\verb~|-X machine.cong-spend~}).}

\p{\strong{Changing a default} is a procedure, not an edit. The owner of the switch sends the evidence: difference reports on all 230 arena tests and four corpora at the default cap, and the measurement rows. The integrator re-runs the gate and the mutants on the published tree, asks for open soundness findings, and measures the candidate against the current default in one quiet window, cedar and Mathlib included. The constant, the list and the facade's test change together. The wave-6 integration followed this and still let wrong accepts through, because every gate compared the machine with the reference engine; since then [an adversarial pass with official verdicts](dec-QN1O) comes first. Since the choice of the present set there is also a rule for the measurement: the geometric mean of the eight warm passes on the JVM launcher decides, and no corpus may be more than 3 \% slower than the set without the switch over at least three interleaved rounds inside one quiet window.}

\p{\strong{History}, in UTC. v0 only until 2026-11-24 16:24; then \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}; the set of the wave-6 integration from 19:41 the same day; the present set from 2026-11-25 02:55. The machine was the default \em{engine} for 54 minutes after the wave-6 integration, was taken back, and is the default engine again since 2026-11-25 03:05, see [that decision](dec-T79V): the default switch set is what a plain run of the executable uses.}

\p{Related: [the gate](design-gate), [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract). Detail: the switch reference, [the instruction-set document](design-isa-document) section 11, [the integration notes of the machine](rep-machine-INT). Source: [the speed wave](ses-CVTE) and, for the present set, [the finalization session](ses-A14B).}
